The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (cifs_sb->root) {
    // dput cached dentries
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `cifs_kill_sb` function unconditionally releases cached dentries when `cifs_sb->root` is non-null, which can happen even if the filesystem mount has failed. This leads to dereferencing issues and memory management problems since the cached dentries should only be released if the filesystem is fully mounted.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (cifs_sb->root) {
    // release cached dentries
    dput(cifs_sb->root);
    cifs_sb->root = NULL;
}
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that cached dentries are only released if the filesystem mount is complete, as indicated by the presence of a valid `cifs_sb->root`. This prevents the release of cached dentries during mount failures, thus avoiding potential dereferencing issues and ensuring proper memory management.

